.TH DCMAKE 1 2026-10-19 dcmake
.SH NAME
dcmake \- graphical debugger for CMake
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY dcmake
.RI [ cmake-argument " ...]"
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dcmake
is a debugger for the CMake language.
It runs
.BR cmake (1)
with
.BR \%\-\-debugger ,
talks to it over the Debug Adapter Protocol, and shows the session in a
window: the source being run, the call stack, variables, and output.
The configuration of a project, or a script run with
.BR \-P ,
can be stopped at breakpoints, stepped one command at a time, and
inspected.
CMake 3.27 or later is required.
.PP
.B dcmake
has no options of its own.
Its arguments fill the command line field in the toolbar, as they would be
typed to a shell, and without arguments the field holds
.BR "\-B build" .
Each start runs
.B cmake \%\-\-debugger
followed by the contents of the field, through
.B sh \-c
or, on native Windows,
.BR "cmd /c" .
So the field is shell syntax, and variables in it, such as
.BR $HOME ,
or
.B %USERPROFILE%
on Windows, expand at each start.
The field can be edited while no session is running.
.B cmake
is found on the
.BR PATH ,
and runs in the current directory, which the window title shows.
Under Cygwin and MSYS2, it must be that environment's own
.BR cmake ,
not a native Windows one.
.PP
.B Start
runs
.B cmake
until it reaches a breakpoint, a message caught by an exception filter, or
a
.BR Pause .
.B "Step Over"
and
.BR "Step Into" ,
when no session is running, start one paused at the first command.
While paused, the source view marks the current line with an arrow, and the
panels show the state there.
Values that changed since the previous pause are highlighted.
The session ends when
.B cmake
exits or is stopped, and its output and protocol log remain until the next
start.
.PP
The status at the right of the menu bar is
.BR Ready ,
.BR Running ,
.B Paused
with the reason, such as
.B breakpoint
or
.BR step ,
.B Stopped
with the exit status of
.B cmake
when it exits on its own, or an error.
.SH WINDOW
.SS Toolbar
The toolbar holds the command line field and buttons for
.B Start
(which becomes
.B Pause
while running and
.B Continue
while paused),
.BR Stop ,
.BR Restart ,
.BR "Step Over" ,
.BR "Step Into" ,
and
.BR "Step Out" .
See
.B KEYS
for what each does.
.SS Source view
A file opens in a tab when execution pauses in it, or with
.BR "Open File" ,
from the Call Stack and Breakpoints panels, or when dropped on the window.
.PP
Click a line number to toggle a breakpoint on that line, drawn as a red dot,
or as a ring while disabled.
CMake moves a breakpoint to the next line where a command starts.
Breakpoints are kept across sessions, and each remembers the text of its
line: if the file changes, at the next start the breakpoint moves to the
nearest line with the same text, up to 100 lines away.
.PP
Right-click a line to add, disable, enable, or remove its breakpoint, or to
run to it:
.B "Run to line"
continues, or starts a session, until execution reaches that line or
something else stops it.
.PP
While paused, hover over a variable reference, such as
.B ${NAME}
or
.BR $CACHE{NAME} ,
or over a bare variable name, as in
.BR if(NAME) ,
to see its value.
.SS Panels
Panels dock, stack as tabs, and float by dragging their title bars, and the
.B View
menu shows or hides each one.
.TP
.B Call Stack
The commands being run, innermost first, with file and line.
Click one to show its line.
The variable panels always show the innermost one.
.TP
.B Locals
The variables visible at the current line.
.TP
.B Cache Variables
The cache entries, named with their types, such as
.BR \%CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE:STRING ,
each expanding to its properties.
.TP
.B Targets
The targets defined so far in the current directory, each showing its type
and expanding to its properties.
.TP
.B Tests
The tests defined so far in the current directory.
.TP
.B Watch
Expressions to evaluate at each pause.
Type one into the empty last row, and remove it by clearing it or from its
right-click menu.
A bare name is looked up among the locals, then in the cache, and the Scope
column says where it was found.
Otherwise the references in an expression,
.B ${NAME}
and
.BR $CACHE{NAME} ,
are expanded, and they may nest, as in
.BR ${${prefix}_FOUND} .
.TP
.B Breakpoints
Every breakpoint, with a checkbox to enable it and a button to delete it.
Click one to show its line.
.BR "Enable All" ,
.BR "Disable All" ,
and
.B "Delete All"
act on all of them.
.TP
.B Exception Filters
The kinds of message, from fatal errors to debug logging, that pause
execution when reported.
CMake supplies the list at the first start, with only the errors on, and
.B "Reset to defaults"
restores that choice.
Changes last until
.B dcmake
exits.
.TP
.B Output
The standard output and standard error of
.BR cmake .
.TP
.B DAP Log
Every protocol message, timestamped, each expanding to its JSON.
Hidden by default.
.B Export
saves the log with one JSON object per line, with the fields
.BR timestamp ,
.B source
.RB ( dcmake
or
.BR CMake ),
and
.BR message .
.PP
In Locals, Cache Variables, Targets, and Tests, the filter field shows only
the entries whose name or value contains its text, ignoring case.
A list, which is a value with semicolons, expands into its elements.
.SS Menus
.B File
has
.BR "Open File" ,
.BR "Set Working Directory" ,
which sets where
.B cmake
runs, and
.BR Exit .
.B Edit
has
.B Find
and
.B "Go to Line"
for the focused source tab.
.B View
lists the panels, and
.B "Reset Layout"
restores their default arrangement and shows all of them but DAP Log.
.B Help
has
.BR About ,
with the version and third-party licenses.
.PP
On Linux, the file dialogs run
.BR kdialog (1),
or
.BR zenity (1)
if it is missing.
.SH KEYS
On macOS, Cmd takes the place of Ctrl.
The function keys work wherever the keyboard focus is.
.TP
.B F5
Start a session, which runs until something stops it.
While paused, continue, and while running, pause.
CMake pauses only as it begins a command.
.TP
.B Shift+F5
Stop: end the session and kill
.BR cmake .
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+F5
Restart: stop, then start again.
.TP
.B F10
Step Over: run the current command, with any function, macro, or file it
calls, and pause at the next.
With no session running, start one paused at its first command.
.TP
.B F11
Step Into: pause at the next command run, inside the function, macro, or
file the current command calls, if any.
With no session running, start one as F10 does.
.TP
.B Shift+F11
Step Out: run until the current function, macro, or file returns, and
pause in its caller.
.TP
.B F9
Toggle a breakpoint on the current line.
.TP
.B Ctrl+O
Open a file in the source view.
.TP
.B Ctrl+F
Find in the focused source tab, ignoring case.
.B Enter
or
.B F3
goes to the next match, and
.B Shift+Enter
or
.B Shift+F3
to the previous.
.TP
.B Ctrl+G
Go to a line in the focused source tab.
.TP
.B Escape
Close the find or go-to-line bar.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B \%XDG_CONFIG_HOME
.TQ
.B HOME
Locate the configuration directory; see
.BR FILES .
.TP
.B PATH
Searched for
.BR cmake .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/dcmake/
The configuration directory, or
.I $HOME/.config/dcmake/
when
.B \%XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is not set.
On native Windows, when neither variable is set, it is
.IR %APPDATA%\edcmake\e .
It holds:
.RS
.TP
.I dcmake.ini
The breakpoints, watch expressions, panels shown, and window position and
size, saved on exit.
.TP
.I imgui.ini
The arrangement of the panels.
.RE
.TP
.IR /tmp/dcmake\- pid \- n .sock
The socket for talking to
.BR cmake ,
where
.I pid
is the process ID of
.B dcmake
and
.I n
counts its sessions.
On native Windows it is the named pipe
.IR \e\e.\epipe\edcmake\- pid \- n .
.SH EXAMPLES
Prepare to configure the project in the current directory into
.IR build ,
then press F5 to start:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
dcmake
.EE
.RE
.PP
Configure another source tree into
.IR /tmp/proj ,
with a cache variable set:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
dcmake \-S \(ti/src/proj \-B /tmp/proj \-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug
.EE
.RE
.PP
Step through a script from its first command, by pressing F10 once the
window opens:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
dcmake \-P script.cmake
.EE
.RE
.SH BUGS
CMake before 4.4.0 has two bugs that affect
.BR dcmake .
On a case-insensitive file system, a breakpoint may not trip when its path
differs in case from the one CMake was given, such as
.I Test.cmake
for
.BR "cmake \-P test.cmake" ,
so use the true case on the command line and in
.BR include() .
And if
.B dcmake
dies abruptly, other than on Windows,
.B cmake
may spin forever and need to be killed by hand.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cmake (1)
.PP
Project page:
.I https://github.com/skeeto/dcmake
.PP
Debug Adapter Protocol:
.I https://microsoft.github.io/debug-adapter-protocol/
